{% comment %}
  blocks/collapsible.liquid

  A disclosure row. Renamed Collapsible Row in the editor; the file and the type
  keep their name so no template referencing `collapsible` breaks.

  ## Still a `<details>`

  The added settings — plus/minus or arrow, a left icon, disabling the accordion
  on one breakpoint — are all presentation. None of them is a reason to hand-roll
  the disclosure with a button and `aria-expanded`, which is the usual next step
  and the usual place accordions stop working without JavaScript.

  ## "Disable accordion" is CSS, not markup

  A merchant can turn the accordion off on desktop and keep it on mobile — FAQ
  content that should read as a plain article on a wide screen and collapse on a
  phone. That is `[open]` plus a CSS rule hiding the marker and forcing the
  content visible, applied at one breakpoint.

  It is *not* rendering a `<div>` at one width and a `<details>` at another.
  That would be two DOM structures for one block, which is the thing this
  refactor set out to remove, and it would also mean the disclosure state resets
  every time the viewport crosses the breakpoint.

  The `open` attribute is still set when the accordion is disabled, so the
  content is in the accessibility tree and findable by in-page search even
  though the toggle is hidden.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign heading = block.settings.heading
  assign accordion_desktop = block.settings.enable_accordion_desktop
  assign accordion_mobile = block.settings.enable_accordion_mobile

  # Open when the merchant asked for it, and also whenever the accordion is off
  # at either breakpoint — a row that cannot be opened must not start closed.
  assign is_open = block.settings.open_by_default
  if accordion_desktop == false or accordion_mobile == false
    assign is_open = true
  endif

  assign toggle = block.settings.toggle_style | default: 'plus'

  comment
    ## The question's typography is the theme's, by reference

    Same three-part arrangement every text block in this theme uses, and no
    fourth copy of the type map:

      the named presets   `.block-text--h4` and its eight siblings in
                          `assets/base.css`, which point `--block-font-*` at the
                          tokens `css-variables.liquid` writes from Theme
                          settings › Typography
      Custom and colour   `snippets/block-typography.liquid`, which emits only
                          what a class cannot know
      the consumption     `.collapsible__heading` in `assets/base.css` reads
                          those properties, with its own values as the fallback

    What it does *not* do is carry `.block-text` itself. That class also sets
    `flex` and `inline-size`, and this span is already a flex item of the
    summary row with a size the row decided. The typography half is the part
    worth sharing; the layout half belongs to the row.

    `default` is the default, so a row saved before any of this existed emits
    no class that resolves and no declarations, and looks exactly as it did.
  endcomment
  assign preset = block.settings.preset | default: 'default'

  assign heading_classes = 'collapsible__heading block-text--' | append: preset
  if block.settings.color == 'gradient'
    assign heading_classes = heading_classes | append: ' block-text--gradient'
  endif
-%}

{%- capture heading_style -%}
  {%- render 'block-typography', block: block.settings -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- if heading != blank -%}
  <details
    class="collapsible collapsible--toggle-{{ toggle }} collapsible--icon-{{ block.settings.icon_position }}"
    data-collapsible
    data-accordion-desktop="{{ accordion_desktop }}"
    data-accordion-mobile="{{ accordion_mobile }}"
    {% if is_open %}open{% endif %}
    {% if block.settings.open_by_default %}data-default-open{% endif %}
    {% render 'spacing', block: block.settings %}
    {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    <summary class="collapsible__summary">
      {%- if block.settings.icon != 'none' and block.settings.icon_position == 'start' -%}
        {% render 'icon', icon: block.settings.icon, size: 18, class: 'collapsible__icon' %}
      {%- endif -%}

      <span class="{{ heading_classes }}" style="{{ heading_style | strip_newlines | strip }}">
        {{- heading -}}
      </span>

      {%- comment -%}
        The toggle is decorative. `<summary>` already conveys expanded state to
        a screen reader, so labelling this would announce the same thing twice.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="collapsible__toggle" aria-hidden="true">
        {%- if toggle == 'arrow' -%}
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'chevron-down', size: 14, class: 'collapsible__chevron' %}
        {%- else -%}
          <span class="collapsible__plus"></span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </span>
    </summary>

    {%- comment -%}
      The inner wrapper exists so height can be animated. Animating padding or
      height on `.collapsible__content` directly fights the `grid-template-rows`
      transition below and produces a visible jump at the end of the open.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="collapsible__panel">
      <div class="collapsible__content rte">
        {{ block.settings.content }}
        {%- if block.settings.page != blank -%}{{ block.settings.page.content }}{%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>
{%- endif -%}

